-- Q3758: SOC Engagements [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Availability, ensuring uptime commitments are met","Processing integrity, ensuring accurate data processing","Security (common criteria), establishing baseline controls","Privacy, governing personal information handling"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AT-C Section 205 and the AICPA Trust Services Criteria (TSC 2017), the security category (common criteria CC1–CC9) is mandatory in every SOC 2 engagement. It serves as the foundation upon which all other optional categories are built.
Wrong (A): Availability is an optional category selected based on the service organization''s commitments, not required in every engagement.
Wrong (B): Processing integrity is also optional and applies only when the organization commits to accurate, timely processing.
Wrong (D): Privacy is optional and addresses personal information handling only when included in the engagement scope.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3758;

-- Q3759: SOC Engagements [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["SOC 1 Type II, testing operating effectiveness over time","SOC 2 Type II, evaluating controls over a review period","SOC Type I, evaluating design suitability at a point in time","SOC 3, providing a general-use summary report"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AT-C Section 320.08, a Type I engagement evaluates the suitability of control design at a specific point in time. This is appropriate for organizations that have recently implemented controls and lack sufficient operating history.
Wrong (A): A SOC 1 Type II requires controls to have been operating effectively over a period, typically six months or more.
Wrong (B): A SOC 2 Type II also tests operating effectiveness over a period, which is premature for newly implemented controls.
Wrong (D): A SOC 3 is a general-use report but still requires either a Type I or Type II examination as its basis.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3759;

-- Q3762: SOC Engagements [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["An entity that uses the service organization''s services for financial reporting","The entity that performs the independent SOC examination","The regulatory body that oversees the service organization''s operations","The entity that develops and publishes Trust Services Criteria"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under SSAE 18 (AT-C Section 320.08), a user entity is an entity that uses the services of the service organization, where its internal control over financial reporting may be affected. User entities and their auditors are the primary intended users of SOC 1 reports.
Wrong (B): The entity performing the SOC examination is the service auditor, not the user entity.
Wrong (C): Regulatory bodies provide oversight but are not defined as user entities under SSAE 18.
Wrong (D): The AICPA develops the Trust Services Criteria and is a standard-setting body, not a user entity.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3762;

-- Q3763: SOC Engagements [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The service organization''s complete audited financial statements","The user entity''s internal control policies and procedures","The services provided, system components, and system boundaries","The service auditor''s detailed testing methodology and procedures"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Per SSAE No. 18 (AT-C Section 320.35), the system description must include the types of services provided, principal service commitments, system components (infrastructure, software, people, procedures, and data), and the boundaries of the system including subservice organizations.
Wrong (A): Financial statements are outside the scope of a SOC engagement system description.
Wrong (B): User entity internal control policies are the user entity''s responsibility, not part of the service organization''s system description.
Wrong (D): The auditor''s testing methodology is documented separately in the tests and results section, not in the system description.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3763;

-- Q3766: SOC Engagements [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Detailed testing results of the subservice organization''s controls","A separate independent opinion on the subservice organization''s controls","The subservice organization''s audited annual financial statements","Identification of the subservice organization and its services provided"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Per SSAE No. 18 (AT-C Section 320.14), the carve-out method requires the system description to identify the subservice organization, describe the nature of services provided, and disclose the types of controls expected to be in place at the subservice organization.
Wrong (A): Under the carve-out method, the service auditor does not test the subservice organization''s controls; testing occurs only under the inclusive method.
Wrong (B): A separate opinion on the subservice organization is not required; it is excluded from the examination scope.
Wrong (C): The subservice organization''s financial statements are not part of a SOC engagement''s system description.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3766;

-- Q3767: SOC Engagements [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The description of control tests performed and their results","The service organization''s marketing materials and appendices","The service auditor''s original signed engagement letter","The service organization''s employee policies and handbook"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per SSAE 18 (AT-C Section 320), the tests and results section details the specific controls tested, procedures applied, and any exceptions or deviations identified. This is the most critical section for a user entity auditor evaluating the impact on their financial statement audit.
Wrong (B): Marketing materials are not part of the SOC report and provide no assurance over control effectiveness.
Wrong (C): The engagement letter defines the engagement terms but does not contain evidence about control performance.
Wrong (D): The employee handbook describes internal HR policies and is not relevant to evaluating control effectiveness.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3767;

-- Q3768: SOC Engagements [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Report it as an operating effectiveness exception only","Ignore it if other compensating controls adequately exist","Recommend management implement it after the report is issued","Report it as a design deficiency since the control never existed"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Per SSAE No. 18 (AT-C Section 320.35), a control described in the system description but never implemented constitutes a design deficiency. It affects both the fairness of the system description and the suitability of the control design, requiring the auditor to evaluate its impact on the overall opinion.
Wrong (A): An operating effectiveness exception applies when a control exists but fails to function as designed; a never-implemented control is a design deficiency.
Wrong (B): Compensating controls do not excuse a fundamental misrepresentation in the system description.
Wrong (C): The auditor must report the deficiency now, not defer the finding to a post-report recommendation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3768;

