-- Q3809: IT General Controls [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Removing all password requirements for user convenience","Requiring password changes every 30 days for tighter rotation","Allowing single-character passwords for ease of memorization","Monitoring for compromised credentials and changing only when needed"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): NIST SP 800-63B (Section 5.1.1.2) recommends against mandatory periodic password expiration because it drives users toward predictable variations. Instead, organizations should monitor for compromised credentials through breach databases and require changes only upon evidence of compromise.
Wrong (A): Removing all password requirements would eliminate a fundamental authentication control and introduce significant risk.
Wrong (B): Shortening the rotation period to 30 days worsens the problem by increasing the frequency of predictable password changes.
Wrong (C): Allowing single-character passwords would drastically weaken authentication and make brute-force attacks trivial.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3809;

-- Q3810: IT General Controls [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["It reduces credential fatigue and the risk of password reuse","It eliminates the need for any other authentication controls","It automatically encrypts all data transmitted between applications","It removes the requirement for multi-factor authentication entirely"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per NIST SP 800-63C, SSO centralizes authentication to a single identity provider, reducing the number of credentials users must manage. This decreases the likelihood of weak passwords and credential reuse across applications.
Wrong (B): SSO does not eliminate the need for authentication controls; it centralizes them and should be paired with MFA.
Wrong (C): SSO handles authentication, not encryption of data in transit; encryption requires separate TLS/SSL controls.
Wrong (D): SSO should be paired with multi-factor authentication to prevent a single compromised credential from granting broad access.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3810;

-- Q3813: IT General Controls [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["It automatically detects and fixes bugs in application code","It prevents all unauthorized access to the source code repository","It eliminates the need for separate software testing processes","It provides a complete audit trail and enables version rollback"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Per COBIT 2019 BAI06.01, version control systems maintain a complete history of all code changes including authorship and timestamps, providing an audit trail. They enable rollback to previous versions if issues are discovered and support code review processes.
Wrong (A): Version control tracks changes but does not automatically detect or fix bugs; that requires testing and debugging tools.
Wrong (B): While version control can enforce access restrictions, it does not prevent all unauthorized access on its own.
Wrong (C): Version control complements but does not replace software testing; testing remains essential for quality assurance.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3813;

-- Q3814: IT General Controls [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Sequencing controls, failure monitoring, and exception handling procedures","Unrestricted access for any employee to submit and modify jobs","Manual execution of all batch jobs to maintain personal oversight","Simultaneous scheduling of all jobs to maximize system utilization"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under COBIT 2019 DSS01.01, batch job scheduling controls must ensure critical processing jobs execute in the correct sequence, complete successfully, and produce expected results. Monitoring for failures with documented exception-handling prevents processing gaps affecting financial reporting.
Wrong (B): Unrestricted access to batch jobs violates segregation of duties and could allow unauthorized or erroneous processing.
Wrong (C): Manual execution introduces human error and is less reliable than automated scheduling with proper monitoring controls.
Wrong (D): Running all jobs simultaneously ignores dependency sequencing and could cause resource conflicts or data integrity issues.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3814;

-- Q3819: IT General Controls [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Requirements gathering and analysis phase","System architecture and design phase","Testing and quality assurance phase","Production deployment and release phase"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Per COBIT 2019 BAI07, the testing and quality assurance phase verifies that the developed solution meets specified requirements through unit, integration, system, and user acceptance testing. This phase identifies defects before production deployment.
Wrong (A): Requirements gathering defines what the solution should accomplish but does not verify whether the built solution meets those requirements.
Wrong (B): The design phase translates requirements into technical specifications but does not validate the implemented solution.
Wrong (D): Deployment moves the solution into production; verification of requirements should occur before this phase, not during it.$EXPL$
